WEBVTT

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.060
Hello, I'm John.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:10.660
This is Müller-Brockmann's Grid Systems. My bible —
and the foundation of how I design.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.800
My first portfolio. Someone did steal my
MacBook, and all my files with it.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.240
I started as a graphic designer, doing editorial
work, like lookbooks for Nike.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.820
Marketing campaigns and posters.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.520
And detailed print work, like paper
sample books.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.060
And for the last decade, I've been
building products at Airbnb.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.150
Ask Airbnb.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.890
Years ago I led design on a zero-to-one
initiative. Airbnb Transportation.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.470
I worked on flight integration on Search.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.940
On-trip needs like airport lounges,
baggage storage, and public transit.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.730
And tools to help build your trip.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.170
Sadly, COVID killed these projects.
But I learnt one valuable lesson.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.190
Planning is hard.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.335
While Airbnb is good at booking stays, we
have strong competitors, like Booking,

00:01:23.395 --> 00:01:24.650
VRBO, and Expedia.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.950
Users also rely on search and
AI to figure out where to go.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.870
Tickets are bought on Google
Flights or Skyscanner.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.550
With various transportation
apps to get places.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.590
While everything is planned
on Calendar and Maps.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.590
What if you could just ask Airbnb?

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.890
I've been reimagining Airbnb with AI.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:03.000
Three things make a trip. Where.
When. And who's coming?

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.370
Or you can just ask, and
the globe responds.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.710
Like — spin.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.710
Or dance.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.610
Or simple yes or no questions
like — are you human?

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.330
Or AI?

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.290
Then ask it anything you want. Like, take
me somewhere that feels like Star Wars.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.690
And it thinks, gathers and gives answers.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.410
You can walk the snowy mountains where
they filmed the Battle of Hoth.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.510
Find the hidden rebel bases in Guatemala.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.870
Or fly to the deserts of
Tatooine in Tunisia.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.890
And ask more questions — like
“how do I get there?”

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.450
And it finds the nearest airport,
and gives directions.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.950
And you can ask “show me flights.”

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.810
And it delivers flight options
during popular times.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.860
You can also ask it to build a roadtrip,
like to New York, and it thinks,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.420
then builds the route —

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:35.530
SF to Reno, Salt Lake, Moab, all the way
to NY, with driving times in between.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.530
Or maybe you're thinking
something different.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.710
Like — a more scenic route.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.870
And it re-routes to fit your ask.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:00.280
Let's ask something more specific like —

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.720
my daughter wants to see giraffes
in the wild next summer.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.830
And it auto fills your dates
and guests, and searches.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.610
Do you want self-drive safaris
in South Africa?

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.170
Natural watering holes in Namibia?

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.030
Or stay in kid-friendly
lodges in Masai Mara.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.130
Then I can dive into the city.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:38.840
I can heart homes I like, and it'll
start collecting my preferences.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.370
And I can browse and heart experiences
I'm interested in.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.530
Choose some flight options for the family.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.850
And book airport pick-ups or car rentals.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.970
Then I tap “plan,” and it
auto creates my trip.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:13.050
If you like what you see,
you can just “book.”

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.450
Then it automatically gets
added as an upcoming trip.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.410
You can tap to see where you've gone.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.390
And if I tap a future trip.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.290
Your itinerary is already built for you.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.050
And you can see all your plans
on a day-to-day schedule.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:03.110
You can sync to your own Google Calendar.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:09.140
So everything you need is in one place.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:17.280
And at any point, add anything
extra you might need.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.750
You dream, AI does the rest.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:32.410
Sketch, sketch, sketch.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:40.900
A big part of how I design is sketching — working
through an idea without expectations.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:45.440
And I want to walk through one of the
prototypes I made years ago.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:50.230
A fluid way to move through long flows like
checkout. No more page after page —

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:57.630
just a stack of simple cards: trip details, a
note to your host, the payment.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.360
Each one right there.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:04.941
You could skip around, move back and forth, and
check only what you needed —

00:07:04.971 --> 00:07:06.780
without the process ever feeling heavy.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:13.309
I passed this prototype to our design
systems team —

00:07:13.339 --> 00:07:17.322
and this thinking directly shaped how search
works today at Airbnb.

00:07:17.352 --> 00:07:20.606
A heavy, multi-step flow, collapsed
into a single screen,

00:07:20.636 --> 00:07:23.920
so you can make decisions quickly,
and with context.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:34.620
Host Calendar.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:43.220
Airbnb hosts manage pricing, availability, and
bookings all on the calendar.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:50.700
The old calendar was confusing and limited — and a
major source of support costs.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:55.780
We started over from scratch.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:03.252
I sketched hundreds of calendars, every
iteration I could think of,

00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:07.680
really trying to understand how to make
something this complex feel simple.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:14.060
We settled on a grid of individual cells, each
with its own nightly price.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:18.640
Tapping a night opens a quick-action panel,
making hosting effortless.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.400
Like blocking or opening nights.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.380
Updating prices.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:31.111
And applying custom settings — like a
15% discount —

00:08:31.141 --> 00:08:33.560
that update instantly on the calendar.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:40.054
And when guests are checking in, every
reservation is right there —

00:08:40.084 --> 00:08:42.480
so you always know who's staying, and when.

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.050
And simple icons flag the nights that matter —

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:53.500
a big tennis tournament in your city, Pride week,
or a holiday like Thanksgiving.

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.839
And those same quick actions reveal the
context behind each night,

00:08:57.869 --> 00:09:00.020
so hosts can price to meet demand.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:06.540
And to make pricing easier, we
built price tips —

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.020
demand-based suggestions a host can
easily apply.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:15.699
And with one tap, a host can apply
the suggestions,

00:09:15.729 --> 00:09:17.780
boosting their chances of getting booked.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:24.700
And a list view for users with
accessibility needs.

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:31.300
And a year view — for a higher altitude, to see
a whole year of bookings.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.681
And a multi-listing view for our
professional hosts —

00:09:36.711 --> 00:09:38.780
the ones running many properties at once.

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:42.700
Without losing any of the power we built
for single hosts.

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.000
For our Experiences and Services hosts,
an hourly calendar.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:53.760
So hosts can see who they're meeting
next — and when.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.060
With simple navigation between dates.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.280
And intuitive little touches, like
drag-and-drop.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.194
Always designed with scale in mind,

00:10:12.224 --> 00:10:15.220
so the tools are the same across all
devices and sizes.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:22.580
All of those surfaces took a year and a half
to create and refine.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.400
Now, it takes weeks.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.500
This is the Multi-Calendar — built for
professional hosts operating at scale.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:36.960
With AI, I've been able to layer in live data,

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:43.030
build in logic so hosts can update all
their listings in bulk,

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.880
and see their seasonality to make
smarter choices,

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:56.680
with an extended quick-action system to make
pricing and availability updates fast and easy,

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:05.280
while keeping navigation fluid and simple,

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:12.080
with quality-of-life improvements, like
automated grouping,

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:20.560
and a powerful, logic-based suggestion system
that automates prices for events and holidays —

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:28.400
removing pain points our users have felt
for years.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:41.050
It's been a joy to build an entire system
from the ground up —

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:45.210
a single system across many use cases,
dialing in the smallest details

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:48.880
while holding the same rigor of craft
across every surface.
